WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.576
Every line has to start somewhere.

00:00:02.576 --> 00:00:03.920
Let's find out where.

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:05.507
Here's the stage.

00:00:05.507 --> 00:00:08.050
The performer is heading for the mark.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.765
On the graph, x is the time in seconds.

00:00:11.765 --> 00:00:15.700
And y is how far the performer still has to go.

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:20.758
Right when the clock starts, no time has passed yet.

00:00:20.758 --> 00:00:21.910
So x is zero.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:26.200
And x equals zero puts us right on the y-axis.

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:29.886
Now look where the line touches that axis.

00:00:29.886 --> 00:00:30.810
It's at six.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:36.380
That point, zero, six, is called the y-intercept.

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:38.449
It's the starting value.

00:00:38.449 --> 00:00:42.430
At zero seconds, the performer is six meters from the mark.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.026
The pool works the same way.

00:00:45.026 --> 00:00:49.318
The swimmer glides three meters before the clock starts.

00:00:49.318 --> 00:00:52.000
So that line starts at zero, three.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:53.901
So here's the trick.

00:00:53.901 --> 00:00:56.949
Go to x equals zero, and read the y-value.

00:00:56.949 --> 00:00:58.690
That's your y-intercept.
